      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Chest pain evaluation centers in emergency departments efficiently exclude acute myocardial infarctions but are limited in identifying patients with acute cardiac ischemia (ACI) without infarction. Short-term prognosis is similar for patients discharged with ACI and for those who had an infarction. Subsequently, for suspected ACI, cardiology or primary care is consulted, providing follow-up coronary angiography or stress testing, but often requiring repeat labwork and resulting in significant time delays. A coordinated approach to chest pain management--an early multidisciplinary effort--provides an efficient and cost-effective approach to chest pain evaluation and management, while reducing potentially dangerous delays in identifying ACI."},
